<task id="135" project="unified-access-control-r2">
  <metadata>
    <title>C1 — one rule set for every contact sign-in: route the CIAM strategy through the SAME evaluator and veto pipeline as the workforce contact plane (Restricted, No Access List, Secure-direct-only)</title>
    <phase>Register-driven (authored 2026-09-30, session 27; contact-plane group, defect C1; re-opens the "every principal kind" claim of tasks 037 and 039)</phase>
    <gate>after-131-109</gate>
    <status>completed</status>
    <rigor>FULL</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>Authorization-path .cs change on the live CIAM plane (/api/v1/external); tags bff-api/auth/security; changes what every outside-counsel contact can see.</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>opus</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>Security/authorization architecture: unifies two plane strategies onto one evaluator without duplicating the veto pipeline, and must keep two different term sets from drifting while one of them is still being retired by another task.</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>xhigh</effort>
    <parallel-group>none</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>false</parallel-safe>
    <parallel-reason>Hot-file rule: edits Infrastructure/ExternalAccess/CallerPrincipalResolver.cs and AccessibleRecordSetService.cs, which tasks 136 and 137 also edit (sequence 131 → 135 → 136 → 137). The same two files are edited by task 141 (CiamContactPrincipalStrategy deny codes at CallerPrincipalResolver.cs:362-373), task 138 (the suppression predicate in GrantedRightsFor and both contact-plane compositions) and task 109 (the junction read); merge serially and rebase onto whichever landed first.</parallel-reason>
    <deps>131, 109</deps>
    <tags>bff-api, auth, security, dataverse, testing</tags>
    <estimated-effort>6-8 hours</estimated-effort>
    <origin>Session 26 verified defect C1 [HIGH] (notes/session26-uac-defects-and-synopsis.md § C1; UC3 and UC6). Owner model confirmed 2026-09-30 round 2 item 3 (Restricted vs Secure) and C9 answer (contacts get only what they are granted). Tasks 037 and 039 claimed "every principal kind" but never touched CallerPrincipalResolver.cs; both were RE-OPENED 2026-09-30 (their POMLs now read status pending, deps +135/+136, with an amendments block naming this task and 136 as the completing work).</origin>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>The same person gets different answers depending on how they sign in. On a workforce token their contact grants pass through the Restricted veto, the No Access List veto and Secure direct-only suppression; on a CIAM token (the external SPA) none of the three runs, so a contact on the No Access List, or holding a grant on a Restricted record, or inheriting an organization grant on a Secure record, keeps full access. Make every contact sign-in go through one rule set.</prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE BFF security engineer — the external-access evaluator (AccessibleRecordSetService) and the plane strategies (CallerPrincipalResolver). You are removing access that CIAM callers hold today, on purpose; treat every narrowing as an access-removal event and keep every fail direction closed.</role>

  <goal>The CIAM strategy's record scope is produced by the SAME composition code as the workforce contact plane — the grant term with Secure pre-max suppression (DirectAccessLevel on sprk_issecure roots), then the deny-list veto (contact and its active organizations), then the Restricted veto (nothing contact-sourced survives) — for all three root types. There is exactly one implementation of those three rules. For the same contact, grants, flags and deny entries, a CIAM principal and a workforce contact-only principal carry identical rights maps.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>🔴 LIVE, verified 2026-09-30 against source.

WHY CIAM BYPASSES THE VETOES. `CiamContactPrincipalStrategy.ResolveAsync` (`Infrastructure/ExternalAccess/CallerPrincipalResolver.cs:339-405`) resolves the contact (`:362-364`), then builds the whole principal from `ExternalParticipationService.GetGrantSetAsync` alone (`:378-380`): projects via `CallerProjectAccess.FromLevel(p.ProjectId, p.AccessLevel)` (`:386-388`) and matters/work assignments via `RightsFromGrants` (`:417-429`), both on the ALL-SOURCES `AccessLevel`, never `DirectAccessLevel`. It never reads root flags, never reads the No Access List, never runs a veto. The resolver routes every ciamlogin.com / Ciam:TenantId token here (`:293-313`).

WHERE THE VETOES LIVE. Only in `AccessibleRecordSetService` (`Infrastructure/ExternalAccess/AccessibleRecordSetService.cs`): Secure suppression inside `GrantedRightsFor(grants, entityType, isSecure)` (`:237-259`, `isSecure ? DirectAccessLevel : AccessLevel`); the ordered post-max pipeline `ApplyVetoPipeline` (`:357-407`: deny list `:375-378`, then Restricted `:391-406`); the deny set from `ResolveDenyVetoAsync` (`:596-672`); the contact plane composer `ComposeForContactAsync` (`:1114-1322`), which calls the flag read at `:1256-1257` and the pipeline at `:1298-1301`. The only consumer of `ComposeAsync` is `WorkforcePrincipalStrategy` (`CallerPrincipalResolver.cs:519-524`).

THE FALSE PARITY CLAIM. `CallerPrincipalResolver.cs:48-50` says "Both planes now source these from the ONE evaluator's answer". They do not. Fix the comment as part of the fix.

WHAT THE OWNER DECIDED (binding, 2026-09-30):
- A contact gets ONLY the records it is granted, at the granted level (C9 answer).
- Restricted (`sprk_accesspermission` = 100000002, `ExternalParticipationService.cs:521`) is a grant-access level on ANY record: NO contact-based access at all; internal users unaffected.
- Secure (`sprk_issecure`) is a separate flag: contact access is allowed only through named DIRECT grants. Organization-wide, standing and derived access do not count. That is why Secure implies Limited for contacts.
- The No Access List vetoes.
The workforce contact plane already implements all four for grants (task 037, task 039).

⚠️ THE TERM-SET DIFFERENCE YOU MUST NOT PAPER OVER. `ComposeForContactAsync` also composes two DERIVED-MEMBER terms that are not grants: standing-grant membership (`:1147-1175`) and organization expansion (`:1177-1249`). The CIAM plane has never had them, and under the owner's model a contact should not get them — "Assigned To *" access is to continue as explicit Collaborate grant rows added by a function (owner Q5), not as derived membership. Retiring the derived terms from the workforce contact plane (and replacing them with the Q5 auto-grants) belongs to task 142 (Assigned-To auto-grants), whose escalation (d) puts the retire-or-keep question to the owner with the recommendation to retire both after a backfill proves no subject loses access. THIS task therefore unifies the GRANT term and the THREE RULES, and must NOT add the derived terms to CIAM: adding them would widen CIAM beyond the owner's model, and removing them from the workforce plane here would drop "Assigned To *" access before its replacement exists, which the owner forbade (round 2 item 5).

THE CACHE PRECONDITION. After this change CIAM reads `DirectAccessLevel` on secure roots. The grant cache (`ExternalParticipationService.cs:1134-1168`, `CachedParticipation` `:1242-1252`, `CachedRootGrant` `:1258-1268`) drops `DirectAccessLevel`, so on a cache hit a legitimate direct grant on a secure root would read None (defect C3). That is why this task waits for task 131 (C3: carries DirectAccessLevel through the cache and bumps CacheVersion 4→5).

THE JUNCTION-FAULT PRECONDITION. The deny-veto subject comes from `ReadActiveOrgMembershipsAsync` (`AccessibleRecordSetService.cs:486`). Today only an EXCEPTION marks it `Unreadable`; a junction QUERY failure (non-success status, timeout) is swallowed inside ExternalParticipationService and arrives as "belongs to no organization", so the ORGANIZATION axis of the wall silently stops matching (documented at `AccessibleRecordSetService.cs:441-455`; #998). Task 109 fixes that one layer down. Without it this task's fail-closed criterion for an unreadable subject-organization set cannot be met on either plane, which is why 109 is a dependency.

TASKS 037 AND 039. Both claimed "every principal kind" / "both planes"; their file lists exclude `CallerPrincipalResolver.cs`, and "both planes" meant systemuser vs contact on the WORKFORCE sign-in. Both POMLs were re-opened on 2026-09-30 (status pending; deps now include 135 and 136; an amendments block with amended acceptance criteria). Their status-notes say they return to completed only when BOTH 135 and 136 are completed, so THIS task records its completion in their amendments but does not close them — task 136 does. ⚠️ At authoring time TASK-INDEX.md rows 037 (:601) and 039 (:603) still show ✅ [done] while the POMLs say pending; step 0 checks whether that drift has been reconciled.</background>
