  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0">Re-verify every anchor above (tasks 109, 131, 138 and 141 may have moved lines in the same files). Confirm task 131 has merged (DirectAccessLevel round-trips through the cache) and task 109 has merged (a junction query failure yields an Unreadable outcome); if either has not, the escalation below fires. Read the outcome of task 142's escalation (d) and task 138's status.</step>
    <step order="1">Choose the entry point: either a contact-principal composition on `IAccessibleRecordSetService` that both the CIAM strategy and `ComposeForContactAsync` call, or a refactor of `ComposeForContactAsync` so its grant-term-plus-vetoes core is one private method with the derived terms applied only on the workforce plane. Decide by the §11 rule (extend, do not duplicate) and record the choice and why in the notes. ⚠️ If you pass the CIAM caller through as a `WorkforcePrincipal`, first check every place that reads `Kind`, `TenantId` or `Oid` downstream (identity caches, membership walks) so a CIAM caller is not treated as a workforce identity anywhere.</step>
    <step order="2">Rewrite `CiamContactPrincipalStrategy` to build `ProjectAccess`, `MatterAccess` and `WorkAssignmentAccess` from the composed rights for all three root types; delete `RightsFromGrants` if it has no remaining caller; fix the parity comment at `:48-50` and the class doc at `:316-321`.</step>
    <step order="3">Tests per the closed set, including the plane-parity test and perturbations.</step>
    <step order="4">Build, full suite, ArchTests, publish size.</step>
    <step order="5">MANUAL live gate against dev (see acceptance); record evidence in `projects/unified-access-control-r2/notes/task-135-ciam-unified-veto-pipeline.md`.</step>
    <step order="6">Append a dated completion line to the existing 2026-09-30 amendments block of tasks 037 and 039 ("amendment n=1 (C1) delivered by task 135 on DATE; evidence: notes/task-135-ciam-unified-veto-pipeline.md"). Do NOT set 037 or 039 back to completed — their status-notes require 136 as well, and task 136 closes them. Update TASK-INDEX and this POML's status; run `pwsh scripts/check-task-status-drift.ps1`.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>Task 131 (C3 grant cache) has not merged when this task starts. STOP — shipping this first makes CIAM direct grants on secure roots read as None on every cache hit (intermittent loss of legitimate access).</trigger>
    <trigger>Task 109 has not merged when this task starts. STOP — without it a junction query failure silently drops the organization axis of the No Access wall on BOTH planes, and the fail-closed criterion below cannot be met. Do not re-implement 109's junction outcome here.</trigger>
    <trigger>OWNER DECISION (carried by task 142 escalation (d)): if the owner decides to KEEP the workforce contact plane's standing-grant and organization-expansion read-time terms instead of retiring them after 142's backfill, the one permitted plane difference becomes permanent, which contradicts the single-rule-set goal. Put the choice to the owner: (i) CIAM also gains the derived terms, or (ii) the workforce plane keeps them as a documented, owner-signed exception. RECOMMENDATION: neither — retire them per 142 (d), because the owner's C9 rule is that a contact gets ONLY what it is granted and Q5 makes "Assigned To" access an explicit, removable grant row. This trigger does not block merging 135 (the derived terms stay off CIAM either way); it blocks deleting the plane-difference comment.</trigger>
    <trigger>The Step 1 comparison shows that routing CIAM through the evaluator removes access from live CIAM contacts in dev (count the contacts and records whose CIAM answer changes). Report the count and list before merging — it is an access-removal event, even though it is the intended one.</trigger>
    <trigger>Task 138 makes `sprk_accesspermission` = Limited (100000001) real by widening the FR-22 suppression predicate to "Secure OR Limited" (reuse, not fork). Because this task routes CIAM through the SAME composition, 138's predicate reaches CIAM with no CIAM-specific code. If 138 has already merged when this task runs, the plane-parity test MUST include a Limited case; if it has not, keep the single predicate as the only suppression point so 138 changes it once. If either task finds it needs CIAM-specific Limited code, STOP — that would be a second implementation.</trigger>
  </escalation>

