  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">FR-21 on CIAM: a CIAM contact holding a direct FullAccess grant on a Restricted project, matter or work assignment does not have that record in its principal (absent from ProjectAccess / MatterAccess / WorkAssignmentAccess) — one test per root type.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">FR-23 on CIAM: a CIAM contact holding FullAccess on record R is denied R when a No Access entry exists for (contact, R), for (contact, an org R references), for (the contact's active org, R), and for (the contact's active org, an org R references) — the four key shapes.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">FR-22 on CIAM: on a Secure record, an organization-only grant confers nothing; the same contact with a direct ViewOnly grant plus an organization Collaborate grant gets exactly Read (suppression is pre-max).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">FR-22 survivor on CIAM: a direct Collaborate grant on a Secure, non-Restricted, non-denied record confers Collaborate rights on CIAM.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Owner model on CIAM: a CIAM contact whose contact or organization holds a standing grant gains NO standing-grant or organization-expansion membership records (pinned by test).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Plane parity: for identical grant rows, flags and deny entries, and with no derived-member data, the CIAM principal and the workforce contact-only principal have identical rights maps for all three root types (one parameterised seam test over the Restricted, Secure, deny and plain cases).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Fail closed on CIAM: a throwing or non-success root-flag read removes the affected records; a throwing deny-list reader or an unreadable subject-organization set — including a junction QUERY failure (non-success status), which task 109 turns into an Unreadable outcome — removes every candidate; no fault returns the old grants-only answer. Each fault is asserted on the CIAM plane through `CiamContactPrincipalStrategy`, not only on the evaluator.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Regression: a CIAM contact with plain grants on non-secure, non-restricted, non-denied records keeps exactly its granted rights on all three root types, and the existing CIAM identity tests (oid-first, email first-login bind, no-hijack) pass unmodified.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Single implementation: grep shows exactly one call site of `ApplyVetoPipeline` per composition path and no veto logic in `CallerPrincipalResolver.cs`; the CIAM strategy no longer reads `AccessLevel` from a grant directly.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The false parity comment at `CallerPrincipalResolver.cs:48-50` and the CIAM strategy class doc are corrected; the single remaining plane difference (derived terms) is named in one code comment pointing at the C9 task.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Perturbation: bypassing the veto pipeline on the CIAM path, reverting CIAM to `AccessLevel` on secure roots, or adding the derived terms to CIAM each turns at least one test red. All reverted before completion.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">MANUAL live gate (dev, no CI), signed into the external SPA as an existing CIAM test contact: (a) a project the contact holds FullAccess on, then set Restricted → disappears from /me and its documents and document content return 403; (b) contact added to the No Access List for that project → same; (c) a Secure project (provisioned through the endpoint) reached only through the contact's organization grant → not accessible; (d) a direct grant on a Secure, non-Restricted project → accessible at the granted level. Evidence recorded in the task notes; test records restored afterwards.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Tasks 037 and 039 carry a dated completion line for amendment n=1 in their existing amendments blocks and both remain status pending (task 136 closes them); this task's POML and TASK-INDEX row agree (`check-task-status-drift.ps1` reports no drift for 135).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Build green; full BFF unit suite green; seam suite green; ArchTests green; publish size reported (both absolute sizes, delta, zip tool, file counts) and ≤60 MB; no new HIGH CVE.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Test scope: cover the stated contract plus the edge cases named above. Anything beyond that needs a one-line justification in the task notes; no near-identical happy-path variants.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>Defect C1 [HIGH], ranked first with C2 in the session-26 fix priority. Compounds with C4 (a Write-holder can mint an organization grant or a grant to a denied contact, and today it takes full effect for CIAM callers); after this task those grants are neutralised at read time on both planes, while the C4 task removes them at write time.

Task 136 (C2, rights-based read gates) edits the same files and runs next: after THIS task a Secure organization-only grant on CIAM yields a None-rights key instead of a full-level grant, and only 136 makes that key stop admitting reads. Do not treat that residue as a defect of this task — it is C2, and 136 closes it.

C12 (fault caching of the grant set, `ExternalParticipationService.cs:857-862`, `:989-993`) is owned by task 132; do not change the grant-set fault path here. Contact identity resolution (`ResolveExternalContactAsync` and the CIAM strategy's deny codes) is owned by task 141 (C7).

Related: tasks 037, 039, 043, 109, 131, 132, 136, 137, 138, 140 (contact-side grant depends on this task), 141, 142; spec FR-21/22/23; design register B-9/B-10/F-4.</notes>

  <execution>
